   "{path?, format?: png|jpg|webp|svg|svgz|pdf|ai|gif|png8|tiff|bmp|tga|psd|txt|dxf|eps|emf|wmf|hpgl|vectorcraft|template (default: from the extension, else png), ppi?: number (raster formats: pixels per inch, stored in the file; wins over scale; without either, the document's raster effects resolution), scale?: number (pixels per point), …the format's options} the selected objects (template layers left out), each in the layers and groups that hold it, whose clipping paths, opacity masks, effects, opacity and blend modes apply as on the canvas (an object of a blend, envelope, repeat or intertwine on its own; txt: the selected type alone), cropped to the bounds the renderer measures for that art (a clip group by its clip) → {path, format, bytes, warnings, bounds} (no path → {dataBase64, format, bytes, warnings, bounds}); warnings say what the format approximated or left out, as for document.export. A key no format takes, and an unknown key inside a PDF or EPS option's object, are refused; a key only another format takes is not used, and warnings name it. Under automation read roots, svg, svgz and the files that carry the native document are refused while the document links a JavaScript file outside them, or while the file would name a linked image's file outside them (an image among the selected objects), as document.export refuses them. In the app, with no params or {}, the save panel picks the file and its format's options dialog follows (Save PDF, SVG Options, the raster options, EPS Options, DXF Options, Text Export Options or an exporter plug-in's parameters; other formats are written at once), whose OK writes the selection: the call returns null while the dialog is open, and OK returns what file.exportAs returns, without format or bounds (Text Export Options: {path})",
