  {:id "document.open",
   :label "Open Document",
   :params
   "{path | name | dataBase64 (a file's path, or its name with its bytes), pages?: \"2-3, 5\" (PDF/.ai: 1-based, default all; one artboard each) | page?: n, cropTo?: bounding|art|crop (default)|trim|bleed|media (the box each artboard gets; bounding: the art's bounds; trim: the bleed box round it becomes the document's bleed, and the artboards are 36 pt apart plus that bleed on both sides; a PDF-compatible .ai or a PDF saved with its editing data opens on its trim boxes for crop), password? (encrypted PDFs; see document.pdfInfo), textAs?: text (default: point type per line, lines wrapped in a frame as area type where they lay out the same, in the file's fonts by name; missing ones are listed in warnings) | outlines (glyph paths), layers?: true (default: optional content groups become layers with their visibility, print state and lock — art that is off comes in as a hidden layer; other art goes to a layer per page; a PSD file's layers become layers holding their pixels, with their names, visibility, opacity and blend modes, groups as layers of sublayers, layer masks as opacity masks, clipped layers and layers in blend modes VectorCraft doesn't have merged with the layers below) | false (one layer per page, without the art that is off; Save then asks for a name; a PSD file opens as its merged image), hiddenLayers?: false (a PSD file's hidden layers come in hidden instead of being left out), importSlices?: true (a PSD file's slices become user slices over its canvas, with its layers or its merged image; slices the file generated automatically are left out, and slices that can't be read are left out with a warning), editingData?: true (default: an EPS, .ai or PDF that carries its editor's own copy of the art opens from it, with its layers, hidden objects, guides, artboards and the art outside them; a .ai or PDF whose type shows opens from its PDF part with textAs: outlines) | \"always\" (as true, and a .ai's editing copy is used even where it draws differently from its PDF part, which can be older; the warnings say so) | false (only what its page or PDF part prints, for print pipelines; a .ai saved without PDF compatibility then can't be opened), legacyNameEncoding?: windows-1252 (default) | macintosh | windows-949 (also euc-kr) | shift_jis | gbk | big5 (the code page names in the editing data of a .ai or an EPS are read in when they aren't UTF-8: layers, objects, artboards, colors, gradients, patterns, graphic styles, swatches, brushes, symbols, variables and data sets, and data sets' text values; warnings list the first three such names as read, and count the rest), colorMode?: rgb|cmyk (the mode the document opens in, its colours converted as file.documentColorMode does, through the profiles the document keeps after the color management policies of edit.colorSettings, grays? as there; default: the file's — a PDF keeps CMYK, Gray and spot inks (spot swatches at a tint) and opens in CMYK when painted mostly in CMYK), grays?: as file.documentColorMode, dxf?: {layout?: \"Model\" (default) | a paper layout's name (document.dxfInfo lists them), unit?, scale? (the ratio: 1 unit of the art = scale drawing units; default: the drawing at 1:1 in its own unit), fit?: false (scale to fit an artboard of fitTo?: [612, 792], turned to the art's orientation), scaleLineweights?: false (lineweights scale with the art), center?: true (false: the drawing's origin on the artboard's bottom-left corner; fitted art's bottom-left corner), mergeLayers?: false (all art on one layer)} (DXF), crop?: as cropTo (its older name), linkPages?: false (true: each page `pages` picks of a PDF or PDF-compatible .ai opens as a placed document linked to that page of the file, as file.place with link places one, on an artboard of its cropTo box (bounding: its art's bounds; a blank page: its crop box; a PDF-compatible .ai: its trim box for crop; on its trim box a page shows its bleed box round the artboard and gives the document its bleed), in a row 36 pt apart (on trim boxes, plus the bleed on both sides), and a layer named after the page; links.embed makes a page editable art; refused for any other file, without a path, with a password or with textAs: outlines; Save then asks for a name), importer?: \"plugin.<id>\" (an importer plug-in's format id, see document.formats: it opens the file, which must have an extension it lists, also one a built-in format reads), params?: {…} (the importer's own parameters, see plugin.info)} → {index, title, format, warnings, restored, missingLinks, modifiedLinks, updatedLinks: [{name, path, ids}]}; the color management policies of edit.colorSettings keep, take off or convert from the profiles the file is tagged with, their notes go to warnings, and a document they changed opens with unsaved changes; any readable format (see document.formats): .vectorcraft/.drawcraft, .svg/.svgz, .pdf/.ai, .ait, ASCII .dxf (each DXF layer with art a layer; blocks symbols; what can't come in is listed in warnings), PNG/JPEG/GIF/WebP/TIFF/BMP (an image opens as a document of its size at the resolution it declares, as file.place sizes it; 72 ppi, 1 px = 1 pt, when it declares none), .emf/.wmf (one artboard, the picture's frame; records VectorCraft doesn't read are skipped with one warning), PICT .pct/.pic/.pict (QuickDraw versions 1 and 2, with or without the file's 512-byte header: one artboard, the picture's frame; shapes, lines and regions as paths, text as point type, bitmaps, pixel maps and QuickTime JPEG images as images; what isn't read is listed in warnings), HP-GL plot files .plt/.hpgl/.hgl/.hpg (one artboard round the art; what isn't drawn is listed in warnings), .eps and PostScript .ai (one artboard, the bounding box: an EPS file VectorCraft wrote restores the document it carries, restored: true; other files are read by a PostScript interpreter — paths, colours and spot inks, clips, gradients, images, type in the fonts named — and come in as their TIFF preview, with a warning, when their PostScript can't be read). A PDF/.ai/.ait or SVG saved with Preserve Editing restores the native document it carries (restored: true; a PDF only when no pages are picked), unless the file was changed elsewhere since or the data can't be read: then its artwork is imported and the first warning says why. Templates (native templates, .ait) open as a new untitled document; a restored .ai keeps its path (Save writes .ai again) and opens on the artboard its editing copy names as active. A file that no built-in format reads, by its content or its extension, opens with the installed importer plug-in that lists its extension (the one whose id sorts first, when several do): it is given the file, at most 128 MiB, and its objects become a new document whose artboard is their bounds; Save then asks for a name, and the warnings are the importer's. Linked images are read from their files (looked for at their path, then relative to the document): missing ones show their saved preview (links.relink), modified ones are read again only with Preferences › Update Links: Automatically (else links.update). An SVG's <image> files (relative links from the SVG's folder) stay linked, SVG files become art, missing ones a placeholder in their box (a warning and missingLinks). An SVG's variable data (SVG Options › Include Variable Data, a variableSets element) gives the document its text variables and data sets, each text variable bound to the type object its docRef names, unless the SVG restores the native document; variable data variable.loadLibrary would refuse is left out with a warning",
