   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/newdoc.rs"}
  {:id "file.package",
   :label "Package",
   :params
   "{folder?, name?: (default: \"<document> Folder\"), copyLinks?: true, linksFolder?: true (the linked files go in Links/, else next to the document), relink?: true (the packaged document links to the copies), copyFonts?: true (the fonts its type uses go in Fonts/; fonts whose licence doesn't allow embedding are left out), report?: true (<document> Report.txt)} copy the saved document as it is now into folder/name as <document>.vectorcraft with its linked files and fonts; the open document doesn't change → {folder, files: [paths relative to folder/name], links, fonts (counts copied), missingLinks: [file names not found], skippedFonts: [{font, reason}], warnings: [nested files copied unchanged or cycles/depth stopped]}; no folder → the same with {name: \"<name>.zip\", dataBase64} (a zip of the files in <name>/) instead of folder. The warnings and the report name a file outside the automation read roots by its file name. Under automation read roots, the package is refused, before any file is written, while the document, or a linked native document or a linked PDF, .ai or EPS file's native document, links a JavaScript file outside them (svgInteractivity; a URL is not checked)",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/package.rs"}
  {:id "file.place",
   :label "Place…",
   :params
   "{path | name | dataBase64 (a file's path, or its name with its bytes), link?: true (a raster image keeps its file's path; a VectorCraft document at path becomes one locked placed document showing artboard `page`, linked to the file and read again when it changes (links.update; saving it in this app updates it at once), drawn as vectors in every output; so does a PDF/.ai page at path, showing its `crop` box, unless password or textAs: \"outlines\" is given, and an EPS file, a PostScript file or a PostScript .ai at path, showing the art false gives; false: an editable copy of its art, as from dataBase64; other files are embedded), text?: {characterSet?: \"unicode\" (UTF-8, or UTF-16 with a byte-order mark, or without one by dataset.import's rule: an even length, at least half of its two-byte units holding a NUL byte, the byte order that reads a line break, valid UTF-16 with no NUL character; other bytes as the platform's 8-bit set) | \"ansi\" (the platform's 8-bit set), platform?: \"windows\" (Windows-1252) | \"mac\" (Mac Roman), removeLineReturns?: false (each block of lines becomes one paragraph; blank lines end paragraphs), removeParagraphReturns?: false (drop blank lines), replaceSpaces?: n (runs of n ≥ 2 spaces become a tab)} (a .txt file, placed as area type filling rect, the replaced object's bounds, or else the artboard less a 36 pt margin), template?: false (onto a new locked template layer below the current layer), replace?: false (swap the one selected object, keeping its stacking place and transform; no at/rect; refused while an object of a symbol instance's art is selected), at?: [x, y] centre (default: the first artboard's centre), rect?: [x, y, width, height] fit inside; aspect kept (wins over at), page?: 1 (PDF/.ai page, or a native document's artboard), crop?: \"crop\" (clipped to that page or artboard, default; an SVG, only when given: framed and clipped by its viewBox, else by its art's bounds) | \"bounding\" (the bounds of the art that shows, with its strokes and effects, as an export measures them: hidden objects and guides left out) | \"art\" | \"trim\" | \"bleed\" | \"media\" (a PDF page's boxes), password? (an encrypted PDF), layers?: false (a PSD or PSB file: true embeds its layers as one group named after the file, read as document.open reads them: an image for each layer and a group for each layer group, with the layer's name, visibility, opacity, blend mode and opacity mask, at the place its merged image would take and clipped to the canvas when layers, hidden ones included, reach past it; never linked: link is then false unless given, and link: true is refused; a file without layers is embedded as its merged image, and so are one whose layers are all left out and one whose layers can't be read, with a warning), hiddenLayers?: false (with layers: true, the hidden layers come in hidden instead of being left out; refused without it), importSlices?: bool (a PSD or PSB file: its slices become user slices, moved and scaled with the placed art, in the same undo step; default true when the file is embedded, false when it is linked, and true with link is refused; slices the file generated automatically are left out), dxf?: {…the DXF options of document.open} (a .dxf drawing: fit fills the artboard under at, else the first; fitted or uncentred art lands with its drawing's artboard on that artboard, else it is centred on at), showImportOptions?: true (in the app, a text file without text, a DXF drawing without dxf, a PSD or PSB file embedded without layers (with two layers or more, or with slices) and a PDF with several pages or a password and no page ask for those options first, and an encrypted PDF given page and no password asks for its password; false places them with the options last confirmed, a PDF on page 1 (or page), and asks only an encrypted PDF's password)} → {ids, name, format, linked, width, height, warnings, image?: the id of the image of a DCS file or PSD/PSB file placed in a clip group, which the Links commands take, slices?: [the ids of the user slices a PSD file brought in]}. A PSD or PSB file that names a clipping path places in a clip group of that path (even-odd), as does its merged image in document.open. Raster images come in at 100% of their physical size (the file's ppi, else 72); SVG, DXF, HP-GL (.plt, .hpgl, .hgl, .hpg), PDF/.ai, EMF/WMF and PICT (.pct, .pic, .pict; clipped to the picture's frame), EPS (clipped to its bounding box; as document.open reads it), a .ai saved without PDF compatibility (from its editing data, as document.open reads it: the art of the layers that show on artboard `page`, clipped to that artboard unless crop is \"bounding\") and native documents as one group, with the images, symbols, patterns, swatches and brushes they use. A DCS file (an EPS file with one program per printing plate, DCS 1.0 or 2.0) is placed only from its path with link (else an error, as are document.open and links.embed): one linked image of its plates mixed into CMYK, clipped by the clipping path its composite is drawn through, its spot plates added as spot swatches in the process equivalents the file gives (a swatch of the same name is kept, with a warning); print separations and Separations Preview show each plate on its own ink, and plate files of their own are read from its folder. One undo step; selects what it placed (unless on a template layer); never touches the clipboard. A PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP image whose pixels can't be read (a file cut short) is an error, and nothing is placed",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/place.rs"}
  {:id "file.print",
   :label "Print",
   :params
   "{preset?: name (as print.setup's: its settings instead of the document's; the saved settings stay), settings?: {…settings of print.setup} (over the document's or the preset's), includeArtboardFill?: true (each artboard's background color prints behind its art; false leaves it out), path?, format?: pdf (default: a print-ready PDF) | postscript (the default for a .ps path: a PostScript file with DSC comments, %%Pages, a page per sheet with its paper size, marks, separations with their ink and halftone screen, the fixed flatness, negatives), level?: 3|2 (the PostScript language level), flattenerPreset?: \"medium\" (PostScript: the preset transparency is flattened with — high, low or a saved one, see flattener.presets.list), acknowledgeLoss?: bool (with path: write over the file the document was read from although reading it left things out), forPrinter?: bool (the PDF goes to a print queue: a page whose paper is turned to landscape is written upright, the art turned a quarter anticlockwise, as a printer driver turns a landscape job; macOS prints a landscape page as it is, cut off on upright paper; paper wide in Portrait and transverse pages stay as they are)} print the document, one page per sheet of paper (per tile, per ink, per copy; with copiesPerSheet, a sheet holds as many copies of an artboard as fit) → {pages, format, path, bytes, warnings}; no path → {dataBase64, bytes, pages, format, warnings}. Raster effects print at the document's raster effects resolution; a PDF leaves halftone screens and flatness to the output device",
