   :params
   "{} thread the selected area type and type on a path (and closed paths, which become area frames) in stacking order; a frame with Dynamic Text has it turned off and keeps the sizes it was drawn at → {ids, dynamicTextOff?: [id…] (the frames it was turned off on; left out when none)}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/threads.rs"}
  {:id "text.thread.releaseSelection",
   :label "Release Selection",
   :params
   "{} take the selected frames out of their threads (the story re-flows through the rest)",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/threads.rs"}
  {:id "text.thread.remove",
   :label "Remove Threading",
   :params
   "{} break the selected frames' threads; each frame keeps the text it shows",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/threads.rs"}
  {:id "text.touchType",
   :label "Touch Type",
   :params
   "{id, start: byte (a character of it), dx?: pt, dy?: pt, hScale?: %, vScale?: %, rotation?: deg counter-clockwise (0: upright)} change one character (a ligature, one glyph for several characters, as a whole) as a Touch Type tool drag does, as one undo step: dx moves it along its line in reading order by changing the kerning of the character before it on the line and its own kerning, and the characters after it stay where they are (an error for the first logical character of a line or at a reading-direction boundary; supported in either reading direction; limited to what kerning from -1000 to 10000 can give); dy raises it (baseline shift, up from its line whatever its rotation, also for the first character of a line and a right-to-left character); dx and dy are document points; hScale (including the object transform), vScale and rotation set its scales and rotation as text.setRangeStyle does. The kerning of the two characters also keeps the character and the ones after it where they were along the line when the font kerned them with their neighbors; a wider or narrower character moves the ones after it by the change in its width. An error for a character of type on an open path that is not drawn because it falls before the path's start → {id, start, end} (the character's bytes)",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/textedit.rs"}
  {:id "threeD.addGraphic",
   :label "Add as Single Graphic",
   :params
   "{name?: string (default Graphic; made unique), ids?|id?} keep the selection's art as a new document symbol, listed among the 3D and Materials panel's Graphics; the selection stays as it is → {name}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/graphics.rs"}
  {:id "threeD.mapGraphic",
   :label "Map Graphic",
   :params
   "{symbol: name of a document symbol, face?: front|back (front; Plane has only a front; the back reads left to right as seen from behind; on Revolve the front is its swept side, which is the only face), x?: pt, y?: pt (0, 0: the offset of the graphic's center from the center of the object's bounds, in the face's own plane; on Revolve from the middle of the side unrolled flat, x around the axis and y along the profile), scale?: % (1..10000; default 100, or less so the graphic fits inside the object, on Revolve inside the half of the side seen from the front), rotation?: degrees (-360..360, 0; counterclockwise), index?: int (the 3D effect's position; default: each object's first 3D effect, its own before its fills' and strokes'), item?: appearance item index|null (refused without index; as effect.setParams), ids?|id?} add a graphic to the 3D effect of each selected object, drawn on that face turned, foreshortened and shaded with it and clipped to it (on Revolve wrapped around the side, and drawn only where its outside faces the viewer); every object's 3D effect must be Extrude & Bevel (on a solid cap), Rotate (the Plane type) or Revolve; one undo step → {ids, graphic}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/graphics.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.disableOpacityMask",
   :label "Disable Opacity Mask",
   :params "{id?|ids?} keep the mask but stop applying it",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.editOpacityMask",
   :label "Edit Opacity Mask",
   :params
   "{id?} edit the mask art of `id` (default: the first selected object with a mask) in place, as clicking the mask thumbnail in the Transparency panel does; refused in isolation mode and in pattern editing mode → {layer}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/maskedit.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.enableOpacityMask",
   :label "Enable Opacity Mask",
   :params "{id?|ids?}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.linkOpacityMask",
   :label "Link Opacity Mask",
   :params "{id?|ids?}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.makeOpacityMask",
   :label "Make Opacity Mask",
   :params
   "{ids?, clip?, invert?} the topmost of the selected objects (or of `ids`) becomes the mask of the others (grouped if several; a layer among several stands for its art, so the topmost object of a layer on top is the mask; an object in `ids` that is hidden, locked or on a locked artboard is refused). One object gets an empty mask and enters mask editing: draw the mask, then transparency.stopEditingOpacityMask; one target (an object or a layer) is refused in isolation mode and in pattern editing mode → {id, editing}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.releaseOpacityMask",
   :label "Release Opacity Mask",
   :params
   "{id?|ids?} put the mask art back above each masked object (default: the selection, or the object whose mask is being edited, which leaves mask editing)",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.set",
   :label "Transparency",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?, opacity?: 0..100, blend?: name, isolate?, knockout?: \"on\"|\"off\"|\"neutral\"|bool (true = on, false = neutral), knockoutShape?: bool, item?: appearance item index|null, target?: \"object\"|\"contents\", aligned?: bool (index and item are rows of the first target's stack: each other target edits its equal one, as the Appearance panel does for a mixed selection; with the art of a graph design in a series given first, rows of the art's stack: docs/mcp.md)} for `ids`, the selection, or the object whose opacity mask is being edited; opacity and blend go to the targeted fill/stroke item (omitted: the Appearance panel's active item, else the objects; when every target is a gradient mesh with points picked by the Mesh tool, Direct Selection or the Lasso, those points take the opacity themselves). With an object of a dynamic symbol instance selected by Direct Selection and no ids, id or item, opacity and blend change that object for this instance only (isolate, knockout, knockoutShape, aligned and target are refused). On a path directly in a graph series (for a fill or stroke item, a series group's id stands for those paths), the graph also stores the opacity and blend mode of the object and of the topmost visible fill and stroke, and on a series group without an item, the group's own opacity, blend mode, isolate, knockout and knockoutShape (exceptions: docs/mcp.md); on a graph label, or a graph's axis line, tick mark or radar ring (for a fill or stroke item, a graph's Axes group stands for the lines and labels directly in it, its Legend group for the labels; when their rows differ, `item` needs `aligned` and is a row of the backmost one), the graph stores that object's opacity and blend mode for every label or line of its kind, and with `item` its topmost visible fill and stroke rows too",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/paint.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.setOpacityMask",
   :label "Opacity Mask Options",
   :params
   "{id?|ids?, clip?, invert?, disabled?, linked?} change the opacity masks of `ids`, the selection, or the object whose mask is being edited",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.stopEditingOpacityMask",
   :label "Stop Editing Opacity Mask",
   :params "{} leave mask editing (the object thumbnail) → {id}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/maskedit.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.toggleNewMasksClipping",
   :label "New Opacity Masks Are Clipping",
   :params "{value?} → {value}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.toggleNewMasksInverted",
   :label "New Opacity Masks Are Inverted",
   :params "{value?} → {value}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.togglePageIsolatedBlending",
   :label "Page Isolated Blending",
   :params
   "{value?} make the page an isolated group (default: toggle): blend modes of top-level objects don't blend with what lies under the page; saved with the document and written to PDF → {value}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.togglePageKnockoutGroup",
   :label "Page Knockout Group",
   :params
   "{value?} make the page a knockout group (default: toggle): its layers (the contents of neutral layers) hide what they cover instead of showing it through their transparency; saved with the document and written to PDF → {value}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.unlinkOpacityMask",
   :label "Unlink Opacity Mask",
   :params "{id?|ids?} the object moves without its mask",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/opacitymask.rs"}
  {:id "transparency.viewOpacityMask",
   :label "View Opacity Mask",
   :params
   "{on?: bool (default: toggle), id?} show only the opacity mask of `id` (default: the object whose mask is edited, else the first selected object with a mask) on the canvas, as greyscale coverage, and edit it, as Alt-clicking the mask thumbnail does; off shows the artwork again (still editing); refused in isolation mode and in pattern editing mode → {on, id}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/maskedit.rs"}
  {:id "type.changeCase",
   :label "Change Case",
   :params
   "{case: \"upper\"|\"lower\"|\"title\"|\"sentence\", ids?|id?} → {ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.compositeFonts.import",
   :label "Import Composite Fonts",
   :params
   "{path: .vccomposite file | data: its text | dataBase64: its bytes (at most 4 MB), apply?: bool = true} read the composite fonts a file holds (type.compositeFonts.export writes them); with apply they join the active document's, a font of the same name (any case) replaced, as one undo step, none when nothing changes; apply false only reads them → {fonts, imported: [name]}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/compositefonts.rs"}
  {:id "type.compositeFonts.set",
   :label "Composite Fonts…",
   :params
   "{fonts: [{name, parts: {kanji: {font, style?: \"Regular\", size?: % 10..400 = 100, baseline?: % -100..100 = 0}, kana?, punctuation?, symbols?, roman?, numerals?: {font, style?, size?, baseline?, hScale?, vScale?: % 10..400 = 100, scaleAtCenter?: bool = false}}, custom?: [{name, characters?: string = \"\", font?, style?, size?, baseline?, hScale?, vScale?}]}]} replace the active document's composite fonts (Type › Composite Fonts…'s OK), as one undo step, none when nothing changes. A composite font sets each class of character in its part's font: kanji (the base: ideographs, full-width letters and digits and anything no other class takes), kana (hiragana and katakana, both widths), punctuation (East Asian and full-width punctuation, quotes and dashes), symbols (arrows, math, shapes and other symbols), roman (half-width letters, their punctuation and spaces) and numerals (the digits 0 to 9); a part left out is the kanji part. size and baseline are percent of the type's size, hScale and vScale scale kana, roman and numerals only, and scaleAtCenter (kana only) scales them from the em box's center instead of the baseline. custom lists custom character sets: each sets the characters it lists in its own font, style, size, baseline and scales (values left out are the kanji part's), ahead of their class; a character two sets list is set by the later one. A set's name is trimmed and unique in the font in any case; its characters are each listed once, at most 8192, none a control character or past U+FFFF; at most 64 sets. Names are trimmed, unique in any case, at most 255 characters, and may not name an installed font; a part's font may not be a composite font; at most 256. Type set in a composite font names it as its family (text.setStyle {font: name}), and the font menus list composite fonts first. A font taken out stays named by its type, which then shows as missing → {fonts}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/compositefonts.rs"}
  {:id "type.convertToAreaType",
   :label "Convert To Area Type",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?} point type → area type framed by its current layout bounds → {ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.convertToPointType",
   :label "Convert To Point Type",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?} area type → point type; soft line wraps become line breaks, a frame of threaded text leaves its thread with the text it holds, and Dynamic Text (Block) is turned off, keeping the sizes it set → {ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.createOutlines",
   :label "Create Outlines",
   :params
   "{} convert the selected text to groups of compound paths (one per glyph); on type scaled unevenly, sheared or flattened, each glyph's stroke becomes a filled path over it, shaped as the canvas strokes it; the type object's own fills and strokes (not its characters') go on paths of its whole outline, below or above the glyphs as its Characters row places them; the group keeps the object's transparency, opacity mask and effects, once; a frame of threaded text leaves its thread with the text it shows; a dimension whose label is outlined becomes a plain group of its art → {ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typecmd.rs"}
  {:id "type.dynamicText",
   :label "Dynamic Text",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?, on?: bool, wordGap?: % of a word space 0..1000 (100), lineGap?: pt -1296..1296 (0)} turn Dynamic Text on or off for the selected type on a path and area type, or turn it on for point type (on omitted: the opposite of the first object's setting, point type counting as off, or on when a gap is given). On type on a path, the layout scales the text by one factor (every character's size, leading and baseline shift; Type on a Path Options › Spacing stays) so that it fills the span between its start and end brackets, larger or smaller, and fits it again after every edit to the text, the path or the brackets; the first character's size moves in steps of 0.1 pt within 0.1 to 1,296 pt, and at 0.1 pt text that still runs past the end bracket overflows. The stored sizes keep their proportions. On area type it is Block: each line as typed (each paragraph, and each forced line break in one; lines are not broken again) is set to the frame's width, less the inset, the gutters and the paragraph's indents, by changing its font size only: its first character's size, in steps of 0.1 pt within 0.1 to 1,296 pt, the line's other characters keeping their proportions to it, with baseline shifts following. Every character's leading is its size plus lineGap, and the layout sets every paragraph with wordGap as its Desired word spacing, its Minimum or Maximum moving out to wordGap when wordGap lies outside it (the paragraphs keep their own Justification… values, which apply again when Block is off; the gaps are area type's, and are kept while Block is off; a gap with no area type in the selection, and no point type it turns into area type, is an error). The sizes are written into the characters and fitted again after every edit to the text or the frame, in the same undo step; at a size limit a line can be narrower than the frame or wrap. An empty line keeps its size. Block replaces Auto Size and Shrink Text to Fit (Area Type Options › Block is the same setting). Off, the text keeps the sizes it was drawn at. Turning it on turns point type into area type with Block on, with the default Area Type Options but the gaps (options kept from an earlier frame do not carry over): the frame is as wide (along the lines) as the widest line as typed with its paragraph's indents, so that line keeps its size and sits where it was (by its own alignment), the other lines are set to that width (when negative indents take more than that line's width, the frame is as wide as the line, and Block makes it larger), and the frame then grows in depth (down, or to the left for vertical type) to hold every fitted line, around any Text Wrap objects above it; the gaps apply to it as to area type. Point type with no line to fit (empty, or only spaces and breaks) is left as it is, and so is point type when it is turned off; a selection holding type in a thread is refused (threading type turns Dynamic Text off, keeping its drawn sizes). When nothing changes, no undo step is recorded → {on, ids, fitScale (the first object's factor; 1 when off and for area type), wordGap?, lineGap? (the first area type's; left out without area type)}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.dynamicTextPreset",
   :label "Dynamic Text Presets",
   :params
   "{preset: \"circle\"|\"arch\"|\"bow\", position?: \"above\"|\"center\"|\"below\", flip?: bool, ids?|id?} put the selected point type and type on a path on a new path of the preset's shape with Dynamic Text on, so the text is sized to fill it (see type.dynamicText). Circle: a circle as wide as the text, the text running clockwise round all of it from its bottom; Arch: an arc of a circle bulging upward over 120°, as wide as the text; Bow: the same arc bulging downward. The width is, for point type, the length on the page of the longer side of the text's bounds, and for type on a path the longer side of its path's bounds on the page (so the same preset again leaves it as it is); the new path is centered where they were, and the shape is upright on the page when the object is rotated or scaled. position (omitted: above) sets Type on a Path Options › Align to Path: above stands the type on the path (descender), center centers it on the path (center), below hangs it from the path (ascender); flip true then turns the text to the other side of its path, running the other way. Type on a Path Options › effect and spacing stay. Area type in the selection is left as it is; a selection holding type on a path in a thread is refused. When nothing changes, no undo step is recorded → {ids, fitScale (the first object's factor)}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.fillPlaceholder",
   :label "Fill with Placeholder Text",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?} replace selected text with placeholder text (area type is filled to its frame) → {ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.fitHeadline",
   :label "Fit Headline",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?} track the first line of area type so it fills the frame width → {ids, tracking}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/textedit.rs"}
  {:id "type.fractionalWidths",
   :label "Fractional Widths",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?, on?: bool} turn Fractional Widths on or off for the selected type and every frame of its threads (on omitted: the opposite of the first object's setting). On (the default, and what a document from before it reads as), spacing between characters uses fractions of a point. Off, each character that takes any space advances by a whole number of points, at least one, so small type does not run together; a character with no width stays at none. The setting is the whole type object, not a range of characters. No undo step when every object already has the setting → {on, ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typecmd.rs"}
  {:id "type.insert",
   :label "Insert Character",
   :params
   "{char: \"bullet\"|\"copyright\"|\"ellipsis\"|\"paragraph\"|\"registered\"|\"section\"|\"trademark\"|\"emDash\"|\"enDash\"|\"discretionaryHyphen\"|\"nonBreakingHyphen\"|\"doubleLeftQuote\"|\"doubleRightQuote\"|\"singleLeftQuote\"|\"singleRightQuote\"|\"emSpace\"|\"enSpace\"|\"hairSpace\"|\"sixthSpace\"|\"thinSpace\"|\"nonBreakingSpace\"|\"figureSpace\"|\"punctuationSpace\"|\"thirdSpace\"|\"quarterSpace\"|\"tab\"|\"forcedLineBreak\"|\"paragraphReturn\" | text: string | code: Windows Alt+0 code (0–255 in Windows-1252, a Unicode code point above: 176 → °, 151 → —) | unicode: hex code point (\"00B0\" or \"U+00B0\"), ids?|id?: object ids (default: the selection)} append to the selected text objects",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.opticalMarginAlignment",
   :label "Optical Margin Alignment",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?, on?: bool} turn Optical Margin Alignment on or off for the selected type and every frame of its threads (on omitted: the opposite of the first object's setting): in every paragraph of point and area type, quotation marks, hyphens, full stops and commas at a line's start or end hang wholly outside the margin there; asterisks, tildes, ellipses, en and em dashes, colons and semicolons hang by half; and A, V, W, Y, v, w and y hang by a tenth of their advance; a left-aligned line hangs at its left margin, a right-aligned line hangs at its right margin, and centered and justified lines hang at both; Auto aligns a right-to-left line at its right; type on a path hangs at the start bracket when its line is placed there, hangs at the end bracket when the line is placed there, and hangs at both when it is centered; no undo step when every object already has the setting → {on, ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typecmd.rs"}
  {:id "type.orientation.horizontal",
   :label "Horizontal",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?} set horizontal writing for the selected type and every frame of its threads; no undo step when every object is already horizontal → {vertical, ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typecmd.rs"}
  {:id "type.orientation.vertical",
   :label "Vertical",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?} set vertical writing for the selected type and every frame of its threads; no undo step when every object is already vertical → {vertical, ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typecmd.rs"}
  {:id "type.pathCenter.bottom",
   :label "Center on Bottom",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?} as type.pathCenter.top, on the path's bottommost point as drawn. The type is not flipped: on the bottom of a path that runs clockwise it reads upside down until Type on a Path Options › Flip turns it → {ids, start, end} (the first object's brackets)",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.pathCenter.top",
   :label "Center on Top",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?} slide the selected type on a closed path along it so that the middle of the span between its start and end brackets lands on the path's topmost point as the object is drawn (after its transform, so a rotated, scaled or skewed path is measured as it appears; on a level stretch such as a rectangle's top edge, its middle; with several separate topmost points, the middle of the shortest part of the path that holds them all), keeping the span's length (a full turn stays a full turn), and set the first paragraph's alignment to Center unless it is Center or Justify Center, so the type is centered there. Point type and area type in the selection are left as they are. Type on an open or compound path, type in a thread, and type on a path with no length or no height are refused with an error that names them, and nothing changes. When every object is already centered there, nothing changes and no undo step is recorded → {ids, start, end} (the first object's brackets)",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.pathOptions",
   :label "Type on a Path Options…",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?, start?: -1000000..1 (below 0 only on an open path), end?: 0..1|null, flip?: bool, effect?: rainbow|skew|3dRibbon|stairStep|gravity, alignToPath?: ascender|descender|center|baseline, spacing?: pt} set the selected type on a path's options: start and end place its start and end brackets as fractions of the path's length (end null: the end of the path, or once round a closed path); on an open path a start below 0 starts the text that far before the path's start, and the characters whose middle falls before it are not drawn (on a closed path it is the path's start), and flip then turns the type to the other side of its path (the path runs the other way, the brackets swap ends); alignToPath runs each character's em box top (ascender), font bounding box bottom (descender), half the height of its H's top above the baseline (center) or baseline along the path, by the character's own font, size and vertical scale (vertical type: half an em either way, or none for center); none given: query → the first object's {start, end, flip: false, effect, alignToPath, spacing}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.resetHorizontalScale",
   :label "Reset Horizontal Scale",
   :params
   "{ids?: [id…]} set the horizontal scale to 100% (as the Character panel shows it, the object's own transform included) in all the text of the type objects in `ids`, else in the text the Type tool has selected (nothing at a caret), else in every selected type object, as one undo step → {ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/textedit.rs"}
  {:id "type.resetTracking",
   :label "Reset Tracking and Kerning",
   :params
   "{ids?: [id…]} set tracking to 0 and clear manual kerning (kerning and kerningBefore) without changing the automatic method in all the text of the type objects in `ids`, else in the text the Type tool has selected (at a caret: only the kerning there, as type.step steps it), else in every selected type object, as one undo step → {ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/textedit.rs"}
  {:id "type.ruby",
   :label "Ruby…",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?, start?: byte, end?: byte, text?: reading, position?: above|below (vertical type: right|left), align?: start|center|end, size?: 1–100 (percent of the base text's size, default 50), overhang?: bool (default true), remove?: bool} set the ruby (furigana) over the selected text's bytes in `start..end` (without them: all its text): the reading over the base characters, above them in horizontal type and right of them in vertical type; `remove` takes the ruby off the range instead; no undo step when the ruby does not change (the same reading and options again, or `remove` where there is none) → {ids, ruby: [{start, end, text, position, align, sizePercent, overhang}…] (the first object's ruby)}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typecmd.rs"}
  {:id "type.scaleTextAttributes",
   :label "Scale Text Attributes",
   :params
   "{ids?|id?} bake each selected type object's transform scale into its character sizes (leading, baseline shift and horizontal scale included), leaving rotation and position in the affine → {ids, changed}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typecmd.rs"}
  {:id "type.size.decrease",
   :label "Decrease Font Size",
   :params "{by?: steps (1)} type.step {attribute: size, by: -by}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/textedit.rs"}
  {:id "type.size.decreaseByFive",
   :label "Decrease Font Size by Five Steps",
   :params
   "{} type.step {attribute: size, by: -5}: five times the Size/Leading increment (typeSizeIncrement)",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/textedit.rs"}
  {:id "type.size.increase",
   :label "Increase Font Size",
   :params "{by?: steps (1)} type.step {attribute: size}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/textedit.rs"}
  {:id "type.size.increaseByFive",
   :label "Increase Font Size by Five Steps",
   :params
   "{} type.step {attribute: size, by: 5}: five times the Size/Leading increment (typeSizeIncrement)",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/textedit.rs"}
  {:id "type.smartPunctuation",
   :label "Smart Punctuation…",
   :params
   "{fiLigatures?: false (ff, fi, ffi → the ligatures U+FB00, U+FB01, U+FB03), flLigatures?: false (ff, fl, ffl → U+FB00, U+FB02, U+FB04), quotes?: true (straight quotes become the Document Setup quotes), spaces?: false (two or more spaces after a period → one), dashes?: true (-- → en dash, --- → em dash), ellipsis?: true (... → …), fractions?: false (1/2, 1/4, 3/4 and the other fractions Unicode encodes as one character, with no digit, slash or decimal point next to them, → ½, ¼, ¾ …), scope?: \"selection\"|\"document\"} the selected type, or every text object with scope document or nothing selected; a ligature or a fraction is written only where the run's font has a glyph for it. Nothing to replace records no undo step → {changed: text objects changed, replaced: {fiLigatures, flLigatures, quotes, spaces, dashes, ellipses, fractions}: how many of each were replaced}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typemenu.rs"}
  {:id "type.step",
   :label "Step Type",
   :params
   "{attribute: \"size\"|\"leading\"|\"tracking\"|\"kerning\"|\"baselineShift\", by?: steps (1; negative steps down), id?, start?: byte, end?: byte, ids?: [id…] (instead of id)} step type by the Preferences › Type increments (size and leading: typeSizeIncrement, pt; tracking and kerning: trackingIncrement, 1/1000 em; baseline shift: baselineShiftIncrement, pt), what the Type tool's Alt+arrows do (Cmd/Ctrl too: five steps; horizontal: ←/→ kerning at the caret or tracking of the selection, ↑/↓ leading, Shift+↑/↓ baseline shift; vertical: ↓/↑ with or without Shift kerning or tracking, ←/→ leading, Shift+→/← baseline shift; mixed selected text objects use the horizontal mapping). The range of `id`, else the type objects in `ids`, else the text the Type tool has selected (at a caret: kerning steps the space after the character before it, or at the start of a line (of the text, a paragraph, or after a forced line break) the space before the character after it (kerningBefore, as text.setRangeStyle), and nothing at an empty line's start; leading its paragraph), else every selected type object → {ids}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/textedit.rs"}
  {:id "type.systemLayout",
   :label "System Layout",
   :params
   "{on?: bool} turn System Layout on or off for the document (on omitted: the opposite of the document's setting) and every text object in it. On, each character that takes any space advances by a whole number of points, at least one, so small type does not run together; a character with no width stays at none. Off (the default, and what a document from before it reads as), spacing uses fractions of a point. New point, area and path type inherits the document's setting. No undo step when the document and every text object already have the setting → {on}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/typecmd.rs"}
  {:id "variable.bind",
   :label "Bind Variable",
   :params
   "{variable, ids?} bind objects (default: the selection) to a variable; text needs type, image needs an image object, graph needs a graph root, art on a locked artboard is refused, and so are locked art and art in a locked layer or group except by a visibility variable. A type object holds one text variable: binding it to another text variable is refused, and the error names the one it has. Variables of other kinds can share an object, and binding an object to a variable it already has changes nothing → {bound, already}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.define",
   :label "New Variable…",
   :params
   "{name, kind: text|visibility|image|graph} define a variable (graph: CSV data on a graph root); refused while the variables are locked",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.delete",
   :label "Delete Variable",
   :params
   "{names?} delete variables with their bindings and values (default: the highlighted one); refused while the variables are locked",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.highlight",
   :label "Highlight Variable",
   :params
   "{variable?} highlight one Variables panel row (a click); what the panel's Delete, Options… and Select Bound Object act on. An empty or missing `variable` clears it. Not an undo step → {variable}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.import",
   :label "Import…",
   :params
   "{path} import a data source file, chosen by its extension (any case): a .csv, .tsv or .txt file is read as dataset.import reads it, and an .xml file as variable.loadLibrary reads it, with their refusals and undo steps; any other extension, or none, is refused before the file is read → the result of that command with `format`: `csv` or `xml`",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.loadLibrary",
   :label "Load Variable Library…",
   :params
   "{path? | xml? | dataBase64?} replace the document's variables and data sets with a variable library's (XML: `variableSets` › `variableSet` › `variables` of `variable` elements with `varName` and `trait`, and `sampleDataSets` of `sampleDataSet` elements with `dataSetName`) in one undo step (none when the document already holds what the library gives). Every data set is deleted, and every variable of the document is deleted with its bindings and values except a variable the library also declares with the same kind, which keeps its bindings; then the library's other variables are defined, unbound, with the kind of their `trait`: `textcontent` text, `visibility` visibility, `fileref` image, `graphdata` graph, and any other or none unknown (no values). Each data set takes a variable's value from the element named after it: a text value from its `p` elements, one paragraph each; a visibility value from `true` or `false`; an image value from a file's absolute path or a `file:` URL (the file isn't read); a graph value from a `data` element (`propertyRow` series names, `values` › `row` with a first `value` key=\"name\" category), read as the CSV graph.setData takes. A value that doesn't fit its kind is refused. Refused while the variables are locked when it would delete or define a variable → {variables, deleted, deletedDataSets, names}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.lock",
   :label "Lock Variables",
   :params
   "{locked?: bool} lock or unlock the variables (default: toggle); saved with the document; a change is one undo step. While locked, variable.define, variable.delete, variable.rename, variable.make…Dynamic and a variable.loadLibrary that would delete or define a variable are refused; binding, unbinding and the dataset commands still run, except a dataset.import whose header would define a variable → {locked}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.makeGraphDynamic",
   :label "Make Graph Dynamic",
   :params
   "{} define a graph variable over the selected graph root(s), named after it and bound to it; each dataset row supplies CSV as graph.setData; refused while the variables are locked",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.makeImageDynamic",
   :label "Make Image Dynamic",
   :params
   "{} define an image variable over the selection, named after it and bound to it; refused while the variables are locked",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.makeTextDynamic",
   :label "Make Text Dynamic",
   :params
   "{} define a text variable over the selection, named after it and bound to it; refused while the variables are locked",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.makeVisibilityDynamic",
   :label "Make Visibility Dynamic",
   :params
   "{} define a visibility variable over the selection, named after it and bound to it; refused while the variables are locked",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.rename",
   :label "Rename Variable",
   :params
   "{name, newName?, kind?: text|visibility|image|graph} Variable Options: rename a variable, with its bindings and its values in every dataset, change its kind, or both, in one undo step (none when the name and the kind are the ones it has). A kind change unbinds every object that can't hold the new kind (a text variable keeps type that has no other text variable, an image variable keeps image objects, a graph variable keeps graph roots, a visibility variable keeps every object) and drops the variable's values in every dataset; locked and hidden objects keep their binding when they can hold it. Needs `newName` or `kind`, and a null or empty one is refused; refused while the variables are locked → {from, name, kind, unbound: [id…], droppedValues}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.sort",
   :label "Sort Variables",
   :params
   "{by: name|object|type|none, descending?: bool} sort the Variables panel's list (a click on a column header) by variable name, by the name of the bound object, or by type (text, image, graph, visibility, then unknown); `none` is the order the variables were defined in, and `descending` reverses the order. Names compare without regard to case; by object, unbound variables come first and variables bound to several objects last, and ties go by name. Panel state: not saved and not an undo step; variable.list reports it → {by, descending}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "variable.unbind",
   :label "Unbind Variable",
   :params
   "{ids?, variable?} drop bindings (default: the selection): `variable` drops that one, else every variable the object has",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/variablecmds.rs"}
  {:id "view.drawMode",
   :label "Drawing Mode",
   :params
   "{mode?: normal|behind|inside} (inside needs one selected path, compound path or type; no param cycles normal → behind → inside → normal, going from behind back to normal when Draw Inside can't draw into the selection; when the Draw Inside target is isolated, drawing or pasting into it moves isolation to its clip group)",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/create.rs"}
  {:id "view.globalRulers",
   :label "Change to Global Rulers",
   :params
   "{global?: bool (default: toggle)} global rulers measure the whole document from one zero point; artboard rulers (a new document's) from the active artboard's top-left corner, or the zero point set for it. Saved with the document → {global}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/edit.rs"}
  {:id "view.guides.clear",
   :label "Clear Guides",
   :params "{} delete all ruler guides and guide paths → {count}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/docmenu.rs"}
  {:id "view.guides.make",
   :label "Make Guides",
   :params "{} turn the selected paths into guides → {count}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/docmenu.rs"}
  {:id "view.guides.release",
   :label "Release Guides",
   :params
   "{index?: a ruler guide's index, ids?: [guide path id…]} turn guides into paths: index releases that ruler guide and ids release those guide paths (and the guide paths inside them); otherwise the selected guide paths and ruler guides are released, and with no guide selected every guide path is released; a ruler guide becomes a straight path with no fill or stroke, across its artboard or, for a canvas guide, across the bounds of all the artboards; index and ids need guides unlocked; one undo step, the released paths selected → {count}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/docmenu.rs"}
  {:id "view.saved.edit",
   :label "Edit Views…",
   :params
   "{name?: string, newName?: string (rename), delete?: bool, edits?: [{name: string, newName?: string, delete?: bool}…] (instead of name)} rename or delete saved views, all in one undo step; names are those before the edit and must stay unique; no params → {views}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/views.rs"}
  {:id "view.saved.new",
   :label "New View…",
   :params
   "{name?: string (default: the first free \"View N\"), center: [x, y], zoom: number > 0 (1 = 100%), rotation?: number deg (default: 0) (clockwise on screen, as view.rotate turns the view)} save a view in the document (the app fills in the current one; at most 25, names up to 255 characters, each name once) → {name, index}",
   :source "crates/engine/src/cmd/views.rs"}],
 :mcp
 [{:name "command_list",
   :title "List commands",
   :description
   "Discover command ids, labels, parameter descriptions and enabled state. Lists engine and plug-in commands, and desktop UI menu commands (ui: true; running a UI-only id headless needs --connect). A command with a parameter schema also lists it as schema (JSON Schema 2020-12) and as inputSchema (the same schema with an object root, the form of a tool's inputSchema). Returns an array. The whole catalogue is large: narrow it with filter (or query), menu, and brief. list_commands lists the same commands; it takes enabledOnly instead of enabled_only and returns {count, commands}. Effect ids (effect.list) are not command ids.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\"filter\": string(\"Case-insensitive id, label or menu substring\"), \"query\": string(\"Same as filter\"), \"menu\": string(MENU_FILTER), \"enabled_only\": {\"type\":\"boolean\"}, \"brief\": {\"type\":\"boolean\", \"description\": BRIEF}}),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "command_run",
   :title "Run a command",
   :description
   "Run an engine command by id with its JSON params. Command parameters follow the registry's descriptions. run_command runs it the same way; it takes the id as command instead of id.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(json!({\"id\": string(\"Command id\"), \"params\": {\"type\":\"object\"}}), &[\"id\"])",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "command_batch",
   :title "Run several commands",
   :description
   "Run steps in order; each edit is its own undo step, or with atomic:true the whole batch is one (named `label`, else after its first edit; `atomic` says which documents). A step's id is a command id, or draw_path / draw_shape with that tool's arguments as params (its result is the tool's: the new object's `id`). A param string that is exactly \"$N.path\" (N: 0-based step index) or \"$last.path\" takes that value from an earlier step's result, e.g. {\"id\":\"$1.id\"}; \"$$\" starts a literal \"$\". Returns completed, failed, results and created (the ids draw_path / draw_shape steps made, in order). Stops on the first error by default. At most 256 steps; kept step results total at most 8 MiB, past which the batch stops with the reason.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\"steps\": {\"type\":\"array\", \"maxItems\": 256, \"items\":obj(json!({\"id\":string(\"Command id, or draw_path / draw_shape\"),\"params\":{\"type\":\"object\"}}), &[\"id\"])}, \"stop_on_error\":{\"type\":\"boolean\", \"default\":true}, \"atomic\":{\"type\":\"boolean\", \"default\":false, \"description\":\"Make the batch's edits one undo step in the active document and in each document a step opens or creates; in a document open before the batch that a step switches to, each edit stays its own undo step\"}, \"label\":string(\"The undo step's name with atomic\")}),\n                &[\"steps\"],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "doc_inspect",
   :title "Inspect document",
   :description
   "Summary of the active document, including artboards, layer tree, selection and history. A node below full opacity reports `opacity` as a 0–1 fraction and `opacityPct` in the 0–100 % scale the drawing tools and object.setProps take: pass `opacityPct` back, never `opacity`. On a large document scope it: depth 0 for the layer skeleton, ids for just those nodes, kind for every node of one kind, limit/offset to page. inspect_document returns the same summary.",
   :input-schema-source "obj(inspect_props(), &[])",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "render_preview",
   :title "Render a preview",
   :description
   "Render an artboard (default the first) as inline PNG without changing the document. max_side defaults to 1024, limited to 1..4096.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\"max_side\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":1,\"maximum\":4096},\"artboard\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"description\":\"0-based artboard (default 0)\"},\"traceViews\":{\"type\":\"boolean\",\"description\":TRACE_VIEWS}}),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "ui_inspect",
   :title "Inspect the interface",
   :description
   "Inspect the connected desktop interface; returns a tool error in headless mode. The same call as inspect_ui.",
   :input-schema-source "empty()",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "ui_screenshot",
   :title "Capture the interface",
   :description
   "Capture the connected desktop window as inline PNG; requires a running desktop app. The same call as screenshot with window:true. max_side scales the returned image down to at most that many pixels on its longer side (1..4096; default: the full capture).",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(json!({\"max_side\": {\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":1,\"maximum\":4096,\"description\":MAX_SIDE_WINDOW}}), &[])",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "list_commands",
   :title "List commands",
   :description
   "List VectorCraft commands (id, label, menu path, shortcut, parameter description, enabled state, and schema and inputSchema for a command with a parameter schema). Includes engine commands and desktop UI menu commands (ui: true; a UI-only id run headless needs --connect). Every editing action is a command; run any of them with run_command (or invoke_menu for a menu path). Coordinates are points in document space, y down, origin at the first artboard's top-left. command_list lists the same commands as a bare array and takes enabled_only. Effect ids (effect.list) are not command ids.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\"filter\": string(\"Case-insensitive substring matched against id, label and menu path (e.g. \\\"align\\\", \\\"Object\\\")\"), \"query\": string(\"Same as filter\"), \"menu\": string(MENU_FILTER), \"enabledOnly\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": \"Only commands that can run right now\"}, \"brief\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": BRIEF}}),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "run_command",
   :title "Run command",
   :description
   "Execute any VectorCraft command by id with JSON params (see list_commands for ids and params). Examples: {\"command\":\"object.group\"}, {\"command\":\"object.align\",\"params\":{\"horizontal\":\"left\"}}, {\"command\":\"file.new\",\"params\":{\"width\":800,\"height\":600}}. Returns the command's result (e.g. {id} for creation commands). command_run runs it the same way and takes the id as id.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\"command\": string(\"Command id, e.g. shape.rectangle, object.group, paint.setFill\"), \"params\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"description\": \"Command parameters\"}}),\n                &[\"command\"],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "inspect_document",
   :title "Inspect document",
   :description
   "Summary of the active document: artboards, layer tree (ids, names, kinds, bounds, fill/stroke; `opacity` as a 0–1 fraction and `opacityPct` in the 0–100 % scale the drawing tools take), selection, undo history, current tool and default paint. On a large document pass depth: 0 for the layer skeleton (truncated levels report childCount), then locate objects with run_command document.find and read them with document.node. doc_inspect returns the same summary.",
   :input-schema-source "obj(inspect_props(), &[])",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "inspect_ui",
   :title "Inspect UI",
   :description
   "UI state of the running desktop app: tool and options, panels, dialogs, view (zoom/center), window, documents, perf. Desktop app only. The same call as ui_inspect.",
   :input-schema-source "empty()",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "select_tool",
   :title "Select tool",
   :description
   "Switch the active tool (as clicking it in the Tools panel). Ids: selection, directSelection, groupSelection, pen, rectangle, roundedRectangle, ellipse, polygon, star, lineSegment, …",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(json!({\"tool\": string(\"Tool id\")}), &[\"tool\"])",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "pointer_gesture",
   :title "Pointer gesture",
   :description
   "Drive the active tool with mouse events in document coordinates, exactly like the mouse would (e.g. a rectangle drag: down at (100,100), drag to (200,150), up at (200,150)). Optionally selects `tool` first.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"tool\": string(\"Optional tool to select before the gesture\"),\n                    \"events\": {\n                        \"type\": \"array\",\n                        \"minItems\": 1,\n                        \"items\": obj(json!({\n                            \"kind\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"down\", \"drag\", \"up\", \"move\", \"doubleclick\", \"tripleclick\"]},\n                            \"x\": num(\"Document x (pt)\"),\n                            \"y\": num(\"Document y (pt)\"),\n                            \"mods\": mods_schema(),\n                            \"backquote\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": \"Backquote held for this event: Selection and transform tool drags transform only pattern paint; Rectangle, Rounded Rectangle, Ellipse, Polygon, Star and Line Segment drags leave copies of the shape being drawn\"},\n                            \"pressure\": num(\"Pen pressure 0..1 (default 1): the Liquify tools' intensity with Use Pressure Pen on, and the pressure the Paintbrush records\"),\n                            \"tiltX\": num(\"Pen tilt in degrees −90..90, positive leaning right (document axes); with tiltY, the tilt the Paintbrush records for Tilt and Bearing brushes. One without the other is 0\"),\n                            \"tiltY\": num(\"Pen tilt in degrees −90..90, positive leaning down the page\"),\n                            \"rotation\": num(\"Pen barrel rotation in degrees clockwise: the rotation the Paintbrush records for Rotation brushes\"),\n                            \"holdMs\": num(\"Milliseconds the pointer then holds still, button down (0..60000): Twirl, Pucker and Bloat keep applying\"),\n                        }), &[\"kind\", \"x\", \"y\"]),\n                    },\n                    \"mods\": mods_schema(),\n                    \"backquote\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": \"Default backquote held state for events: Selection and transform tool drags transform only pattern paint; Rectangle, Rounded Rectangle, Ellipse, Polygon, Star and Line Segment drags leave copies of the shape being drawn\"},\n                }),\n                &[\"events\"],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "draw_path",
   :title "Draw path",
   :description
   "Create a path from points ([[x,y],…] or [{x,y,in?:[x,y],out?:[x,y]},…] with Bézier handles) or SVG path data `d`, then apply optional paint, stroke dash/cap/join, opacity, name and live effects. Omitted paint fields inherit the current new-art appearance (initially white fill and 1 pt black stroke). For a filled path without an outline, pass an explicit fill and stroke:\"none\". Returns the new object id.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                with_draw_options(json!({\n                    \"points\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"description\": \"Anchors: [x,y] pairs or {x, y, in?, out?, smooth?} objects\", \"items\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"number\"}, \"minItems\": 2}, {\"type\": \"object\"}]}},\n                    \"d\": string(\"SVG path data (alternative to points), e.g. \\\"M0 0 L100 0 L50 80 Z\\\"\"),\n                    \"closed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": \"Close the path (points only)\"},\n                    \"fill\": paint_schema(\"Fill\"),\n                    \"stroke\": paint_schema(\"Stroke\"),\n                    \"strokeWidth\": num(\"Stroke weight in pt\"),\n                })),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "draw_shape",
   :title "Draw shape",
   :description
   "Create a live shape and apply optional paint. rectangle/ellipse: x, y, width, height (rectangle: radius = corner radius; ellipse: anchors, default 4, where any other count from 2 to 1000 draws a plain path rather than a live shape). polygon: cx, cy, radius, sides. star: cx, cy, radius1, radius2, points. line: x1, y1, x2, y2. Omitted paint fields inherit the current new-art appearance (initially white fill and 1 pt black stroke); lines start with no fill and a visible stroke. For a filled shape without an outline, pass an explicit fill and stroke:\"none\". Stroke dash/cap/join, opacity, name and live effects apply to the new shape as in draw_path. Returns the new object id.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                with_draw_options(json!({\n                    \"shape\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"rectangle\", \"ellipse\", \"polygon\", \"star\", \"line\"]},\n                    \"x\": num(\"Left (rectangle/ellipse)\"),\n                    \"y\": num(\"Top (rectangle/ellipse)\"),\n                    \"width\": num(\"Width (rectangle/ellipse)\"),\n                    \"height\": num(\"Height (rectangle/ellipse)\"),\n                    \"radius\": num(\"Corner radius (rectangle) or radius (polygon)\"),\n                    \"anchors\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"minimum\": 2, \"maximum\": 1000, \"description\": \"Ellipse anchors, spaced evenly by angle (default 4, a live ellipse; any other count draws a plain path)\"},\n                    \"cx\": num(\"Centre x (polygon/star)\"),\n                    \"cy\": num(\"Centre y (polygon/star)\"),\n                    \"sides\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"minimum\": 3, \"description\": \"Polygon sides (default 6)\"},\n                    \"radius1\": num(\"Star outer radius\"),\n                    \"radius2\": num(\"Star inner radius\"),\n                    \"points\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"minimum\": 3, \"description\": \"Star points (default 5)\"},\n                    \"rotation\": num(\"Rotation in degrees clockwise about the centre (rectangle/ellipse: 0 upright; polygon/star: 0 a corner or point straight up)\"),\n                    \"x1\": num(\"Line start x\"),\n                    \"y1\": num(\"Line start y\"),\n                    \"x2\": num(\"Line end x\"),\n                    \"y2\": num(\"Line end y\"),\n                    \"fill\": paint_schema(\"Fill\"),\n                    \"stroke\": paint_schema(\"Stroke\"),\n                    \"strokeWidth\": num(\"Stroke weight in pt\"),\n                })),\n                &[\"shape\"],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "set_paint",
   :title "Set paint",
   :description
   "Set fill, stroke and/or stroke weight on the selection (or `ids`) and as the default for new art. Returns {applied, objects}: `objects` counts the objects painted, as paint.setFill, paint.setStroke and stroke.set report them (the largest of their counts); when it is 0, a `note` says that only the default for new art changed.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"fill\": paint_schema(\"Fill\"),\n                    \"stroke\": paint_schema(\"Stroke\"),\n                    \"strokeWidth\": num(\"Stroke weight in pt\"),\n                    \"ids\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"description\": \"Object ids (default: the selection)\"},\n                }),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "press_key",
   :title "Press key",
   :description
   "Send a key press with modifiers, e.g. {key:\"Z\", mods:{cmd:true}} (undo), {key:\"Escape\"}, {key:\"Enter\"}. In the desktop app it goes through the real keyboard path; headless, it runs the command or tool bound to that shortcut.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\"key\": string(\"Key name: A–Z, 0–9, Enter, Escape, Delete, Backspace, Tab, Space, Left/Right/Up/Down, [ ]…\"), \"mods\": mods_schema()}),\n                &[\"key\"],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "type_text",
   :title "Type text",
   :description
   "Type text into the focused widget (text tool, dialog field). Desktop app only.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(json!({\"text\": string(\"Text to type\")}), &[\"text\"])",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "answer_dialog",
   :title "Answer dialog",
   :description
   "Set fields of the desktop app's open dialog in order, then confirm (OK) or cancel it, as the control protocol's ui.dialog.set, ui.dialog.confirm and ui.dialog.cancel do. The app stores all the fields or none and answers that same dialog in the same request (ui.dialog.set with action). When the app refuses the ui.dialog.set, for example because no dialog is open, the call is an error and the dialog is not confirmed or canceled. inspect_ui shows the open dialog and its fields (ui.dialog). After invoke_menu app.quit or file.close replies {\"pending\":\"saveChanges\"}: {action:\"confirm\"} saves, {set:[{field:\"discard\",value:true}],action:\"confirm\"} closes without saving, {action:\"cancel\"} cancels. Returns {dialog, result}: the dialog after the fields were set, and the reply to confirm or cancel. Desktop app only.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"set\": {\n                        \"type\": \"array\",\n                        \"maxItems\": MAX_DIALOG_FIELDS,\n                        \"items\": obj(json!({\"field\": string(\"Dialog field\"), \"value\": {\"description\": \"The field's new value (any JSON; default null)\"}}), &[\"field\"]),\n                        \"description\": \"Fields to set, in order (one ui.dialog.set that sets all of them or none)\",\n                    },\n                    \"action\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"confirm\", \"cancel\"], \"description\": \"Then confirm (OK) or cancel the dialog the fields were set in\"},\n                }),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "invoke_menu",
   :title "Invoke menu",
   :description
   "Invoke a menu item by its command id (same ids as list_commands, including UI-only commands like view.zoomIn or window.* in the desktop app).",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(json!({\"command\": string(\"Menu command id\"), \"params\": {\"type\": \"object\"}}), &[\"command\"])",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "open_panel",
   :title "Open panel",
   :description
   "Open a panel in the desktop app's dock by id (layers, swatches, stroke, align, pathfinder, transform, …; case-insensitive, display labels like \"Layers\" work too). Desktop app only.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(json!({\"panel\": string(\"Panel id or display label\")}), &[\"panel\"])",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "screenshot",
   :title "Screenshot",
   :description
   "Render the active artboard to PNG and return it as an image (optionally also saving it to `path`). With window:true (desktop app only) captures the whole app window instead, as ui_screenshot does; max_side then scales the returned image down (a file written to `path` keeps the full capture).",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"path\": string(\"Also write the PNG here\"),\n                    \"scale\": num(\"Pixels per point (default 1)\"),\n                    \"artboard\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"minimum\": 0, \"description\": \"Artboard index (default 0)\"},\n                    \"window\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": \"Capture the app window (desktop app only)\"},\n                    \"max_side\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"minimum\": 1, \"maximum\": 4096, \"description\": MAX_SIDE_WINDOW},\n                    \"traceViews\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": TRACE_VIEWS},\n                }),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "open_file",
   :title "Open file",
   :description &format!,
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"path\": string(\"File path\"),\n                    \"importer\": string(\"An importer plug-in's format id, plugin.<id> (see run_command document.formats), which must list the file's extension (default: the built-in format that reads the file, else the first importer by id that lists its extension)\"),\n                    \"params\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"description\": \"The importer plug-in's parameters (see run_command plugin.list; default: the defaults its manifest declares)\"},\n                }),\n                &[\"path\"],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "save_file",
   :title "Save file",
   :description &format!,
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"path\": string(\"Destination; its extension picks the format (default: the document's own file)\"),\n                    \"compress\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": \"Native files: gzip the file (default: the useCompression preference)\"},\n                    \"version\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"minimum\": 1, \"maximum\": vectorcraft_format::VERSION, \"description\": \"Native files: the format version to write (default: the current one; 1 and 2 are never compressed)\"},\n                    \"preview\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": \"Native files: embed a PNG of the first artboard, at most 256 px (default false)\"},\n                    \"precision\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"minimum\": 0, \"maximum\": vectorcraft_format::MAX_PRECISION, \"description\": \"Native files: round path anchors and handles to this many decimal places, a smaller file; colors, transforms and type stay exact (default: exact)\"},\n                    \"options\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"description\": \"The format's save options, passed through (see run_command document.formats); default: as last saved\"},\n                }),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "export",
   :title "Export",
   :description
   "Export the active document with the engine's document.export (the same bytes in the app and headless), in any format of the `format` enum: vectors (svg/svgz with the artboard as viewBox, eps, dxf, emf, wmf, hpgl pen moves for plotters and cutters), pdf and ai (one page per artboard: all, or `artboard` / `range`; ai is the PDF-compatible .ai file that Save writes, which carries the document when every artboard is written in order), rasters (one rendered artboard: png, jpg, webp, gif, png8 as an indexed .png, tiff, bmp, tga, psd with its layers), txt (the stories, back to front), vectorcraft or template (a native copy or template; the document keeps its path). run_command document.formats lists each format's options. For SVG/SVGZ, top-level `outlineText: true` writes text as paths; DXF takes it too (its type is outlined anyway unless `preserve: editability`). PDF instead uses `options.advanced.outlineText: true`, default false (live text). `selection: true` exports only the selected objects, cropped to their bounds. Template layers are left out; live effects are kept (geometry baked, SVG filters for shadows/glows/blur). Without `path` the bytes come back as dataBase64.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"format\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": FORMATS.iter().filter(|f| f.write).map(|f| f.id).collect::<Vec<_>>(), \"description\": \"Default: from the path's extension\"},\n                    \"path\": string(\"Destination file (omit to get dataBase64)\"),\n                    \"scale\": num(\"Raster pixels per point (without scale or options.ppi, uses the document's raster effects resolution; options.ppi wins)\"),\n                    \"artboard\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"minimum\": 0, \"description\": \"0-based artboard (default: the first; PDF and .ai default: all)\"},\n                    \"range\": string(\"1-based artboards such as \\\"1-3, 5\\\" (PDF and .ai: one page each; SVG and SVGZ: one file each; EPS: one file each, after a central file of the whole document when there are two or more and art shows; rasters, DXF, EMF, WMF and HP-GL: one, or one file each with options.useArtboards: true)\"),\n                    \"selection\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": \"Export only the selection, cropped to it\"},\n                    \"outlineText\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": \"SVG/SVGZ: true writes text as outlines (viewable without the fonts). DXF takes it too (type outlined anyway unless preserve: editability). PDF uses options.advanced.outlineText instead (default false: live text)\"},\n                    \"options\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"description\": \"More format options, passed through (see run_command document.formats), e.g. {\\\"quality\\\": 80} for jpg or {\\\"advanced\\\": {\\\"outlineText\\\": true}} for PDF text as outlines (default false: live text)\"},\n                }),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "add_text",
   :title "Add text",
   :description
   "Create point type at (x, y) (the first baseline), area type when width/height are given (`fit` autoHeight grows the frame to the text, shrinkText scales the text down to fit), or type in/on an existing path (`path` id + `mode` area|onPath; `pathEffect` rainbow|skew|3dRibbon|stairStep|gravity). Returns the text object id, with `overflow: true` when the text doesn't fit its frame or path (the lines past it aren't drawn: enlarge the frame, shrink the text or give `fit`).",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"text\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                    \"x\": num(\"Baseline origin x (area type: left; point type: where the line starts, or its centre or end with justify center/right)\"),\n                    \"y\": num(\"First baseline y (area type: top)\"),\n                    \"width\": num(\"Area type frame width\"),\n                    \"height\": num(\"Area type frame height\"),\n                    \"vertical\": {\"type\": \"boolean\", \"description\": \"Vertical writing for point, area or path type (default false, horizontal), as text.create / text.createInPath\"},\n                    \"fit\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"none\", \"autoHeight\", \"shrinkText\"], \"description\": \"Area type: keep the frame (none), fit its height to the text (autoHeight) or scale overflowing text down (shrinkText)\"},\n                    \"fitMinPercent\": num(\"shrinkText: the smallest scale in percent, 10 to 100 (default 50)\"),\n                    \"path\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"description\": \"Existing path id to flow the text in (area) or along (onPath)\"},\n                    \"mode\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"area\", \"onPath\"]},\n                    \"pathEffect\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"rainbow\", \"skew\", \"3dRibbon\", \"stairStep\", \"gravity\"]},\n                    \"size\": num(\"Font size in pt\"),\n                    \"font\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"Font family\"},\n                    \"color\": paint_schema(\"Text colour (#rrggbb)\"),\n                    \"justify\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"auto\", \"left\", \"center\", \"right\", \"justifyAll\"], \"description\": \"Paragraph alignment (default auto: the start of the text's direction). Point type lines up on x: center centres each line on it, right ends it there\"},\n                }),\n                &[\"text\"],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "apply_effect",
   :title "Apply live effect",
   :description
   "Append a live effect (Effect menu) to the selection or `ids`. Omit `effect` to get the catalogue (ids, parameters, defaults). Examples: stylize.dropShadow {x, y, blur, opacity}, distort.roughen, warp.arc {bend}, path.offsetPath {offset}, pathfinder.add (on groups), blur.gaussian {radius}.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"effect\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"Effect id (see the catalogue)\"},\n                    \"params\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"description\": \"Effect parameters; missing keys take the dialog defaults\"},\n                    \"ids\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}},\n                }),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "pathfinder",
   :title "Pathfinder",
   :description
   "Combine the selected (or `ids`) objects destructively, back to front: unite, minusFront, intersect, exclude, divide, trim, merge, crop, outline, minusBack. For a non-destructive version apply the pathfinder.* effect to a group.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"operation\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"unite\", \"minusFront\", \"intersect\", \"exclude\", \"divide\", \"trim\", \"merge\", \"crop\", \"outline\", \"minusBack\"]},\n                    \"ids\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}},\n                }),\n                &[\"operation\"],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "transform",
   :title "Transform",
   :description
   "Move, rotate, scale, reflect or shear the selection (or `ids`); several may be combined and run in that order. Angles in degrees (counter-clockwise), scale in %. Scale and reflect act along the selection's bounding box, turned with rotated objects until Reset Bounding Box. `origin` [x,y] defaults to the selection centre; `copy` transforms a copy.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"ids\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}},\n                    \"dx\": num(\"Move right\"),\n                    \"dy\": num(\"Move down\"),\n                    \"rotate\": num(\"Rotation in degrees counter-clockwise\"),\n                    \"scale\": num(\"Uniform scale in %\"),\n                    \"scaleX\": num(\"Horizontal scale in %\"),\n                    \"scaleY\": num(\"Vertical scale in %\"),\n                    \"reflect\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"vertical\", \"horizontal\"]},\n                    \"shear\": num(\"Shear angle in degrees (horizontal axis: positive moves the top to the right, turning the vertical edges clockwise)\"),\n                    \"origin\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"number\"}, \"minItems\": 2, \"maxItems\": 2},\n                    \"copy\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"},\n                }),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "create_graph",
   :title "Create graph",
   :description
   "Create a graph (graph tools) in the plot rectangle. Data as `csv` (first row: empty cell then series names; then one row per category: label, values…) or `series`/`categories`/`rows`. An empty CSV cell or a null in `rows` is a blank value (no column; lines break around it); a number in straight quotes is a label. Edit later with run_command graph.setData / graph.setType.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"type\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"column\", \"stackedColumn\", \"bar\", \"stackedBar\", \"line\", \"area\", \"scatter\", \"pie\", \"radar\"]},\n                    \"x\": num(\"Plot left\"),\n                    \"y\": num(\"Plot top\"),\n                    \"width\": num(\"Plot width\"),\n                    \"height\": num(\"Plot height\"),\n                    \"csv\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                    \"series\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                    \"categories\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                    \"rows\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": [\"number\", \"null\"]}}},\n                }),\n                &[\"x\", \"y\", \"width\", \"height\"],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "text_wrap",
   :title "Text wrap",
   :description
   "Make the selected (or `ids`) objects wrap objects: area type below them in the same layer flows around them (offset in pt, invert = flow inside). `release: true` removes the wrap. An offset outside -1000 to 1000 or not a number, or an invert or release that is not true or false, is an error and changes nothing.",
   :input-schema-source
   "obj(\n                json!({\n                    \"ids\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}},\n                    \"offset\": num(\"Gap between text and object in pt, -1000 to 1000 (default 6 pt); a negative gap lets text that far into the object\"),\n                    \"invert\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"},\n                    \"release\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"},\n                }),\n                &[],\n            )",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "undo",
   :title "Undo",
   :description
   "Undo the last change (Edit → Undo). redo reapplies it.",
   :input-schema-source "empty()",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}
  {:name "redo",
   :title "Redo",
   :description
   "Redo the last undone change (Edit → Redo): the change the last undo took back.",
   :input-schema-source "empty()",
   :source "crates/mcp/src/tools/definitions.rs"}],
